# mldr-sig65-unblock-count.patch: unblock the native real-time signals 65/66 at trap setup (the guest sigexc_setup2 cannot name them in its 8-byte mask) and count trampoline entries for 65/66 (printed as [sig65-count] only when DARLING_SIG65_COUNT is set in the guest environment), to compare with ring kind 5 (signals sent) and kind 14 (watchdog re-sends).
../.. mldr-sig65-unblock-count.patch
# xnu-sigexc-setup2-no-shift64.patch (verified: guest libsystem_kernel relinked with relink-libsystem-kernel-dirty.sh, sigexc_setup2 is empty in the binary, sig-reentry 3/3 and busy info 1 pass): sigexc_setup2 built its unblock mask with 1ull << 64 (undefined; SIGHUP on x86); signals 65/66 are not expressible in the guest 8-byte mask, mldr unblocks them natively. Not rebuilt into the guest libsystem_kernel in the probe runs (compile-only change).
xnu xnu-sigexc-setup2-no-shift64.patch
# mldr-traplog-nostdio.patch: PROBE_TRAP_LOG with blocking stdio output (fprintf(stderr) under the FILE lock) deadlocks with thread_suspend: the suspended thread keeps the stderr lock, the suspender blocks on it and never sends thread_resume (host state of the hung runs: suspender and watchdog in do_lock_umutex, worker in recvmsg). The hot-path trap-log lines (sendmsg/recvmsg/kprintf-text/sigprocmask) are now written lock-free with write(2). Other fprintf(stderr) under DARLING_TRAP_LOG remain: do not use a trap-log run as an acceptance run of the suspend fix unless the run was made with this patch; acceptance runs: debug without PROBE_TRAP_LOG.
../.. mldr-traplog-nostdio.patch
# mldr-rpc-socket-close-order.patch: __mldr_close_rpc_socket released the fd number (socket_bitmap_put) before unlinking /tmp/darling-mldr-<pid>-<fd>; a new thread could take the number, bind the same name and then lose it to the late unlink.
../.. mldr-rpc-socket-close-order.patch
# darlingserver-memaccess-detach-signal.patch: ROOT CAUSE of the lost suspend signals (about 3 percent, always sent while the server copied guest memory for the thread last call, usually mach_port_allocate): process_vm_readv/writev is shimmed with PT_ATTACH, waitpid, PT_IO, PT_DETACH; a signal sent to a guest thread in that window becomes a ptrace stop, the wait status names it instead of SIGSTOP, and PT_DETACH with signal 0 cancels it. The status signal is now handed back at detach.
darlingserver darlingserver-memaccess-detach-signal.patch
